
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Codex is installed, so the approach gate was available — but the two clarifying answers collapsed the architectural
  space (runtime selection + global script tag is now fixed), leaving one obvious shape. I'm skipping the gate rather
  than spending a 10-minute call on a settled design. Say so if you want Codex's independent take anyway.

  Last question before I present the design. [1mThe fork:[0m how a developer forces a non-matching environment — e.g. pointing
  a locally-served page at staging.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m override[0m — set [38;5;153mlocalStorage.appEnv = "staging"[39m once in devtools; it persists across reloads until
    cleared. Buys you: sticky, survives refresh, invisible in the URL. Costs: persistent state that's easy to forget you
    set, which produces confusing "why is prod hitting staging" moments later.
  - [1mURL query param[0m ([38;5;153m?env=staging[39m) — buys you: self-documenting, shareable as a link, vanishes when you drop the param.
    Costs: not sticky across navigation, and it's user-visible on a login page, so anyone can flip the endpoint by
    editing the URL.
  - [1mBoth, param winning over localStorage[0m — covers both workflows. Costs: two override sources means a precedence rule
    to reason about when they disagree.
  - [1mNo override, hostname only[0m — buys you: nothing to misconfigure. Costs: you can't test staging from localhost without
    editing the file, which is the problem you asked me to solve.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m override.[0m It's sticky, which matches the actual workflow (point my local page at
  staging and leave it there for the afternoon), and it keeps the environment switch out of a URL on a login form. This
  one is cheap to change later either way.
